<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> It's My Family!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize when written, without visual support, not only their own name but also the names of classmates or family members.
            Reading: Point to simple words in the text that identify objects or characters in a picture book.
            Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize family-related words, including own name, in written texts (e.g., "mom," "dad").
            Can match names of family members to pictures in books (e.g., matching the word "father" to a picture of a father in a family picture book).
            Can identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound (e.g., identifying the sound of the letter "S" in the word "sister" when shown a picture of a sister).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts with the "Family Freeze Dance." The teacher plays music and shows flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandma, grandpa). When the music stops, the teacher holds up a flashcard with the *written word* (e.g., "MOM") and students freeze in a pose representing that family member, or point to the matching picture on the board. This activates vocabulary and visually connects words to images.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards with only the written names of family members (e.g., "MOM," "DAD," "SISTER"). The teacher points to each word, pronounces it clearly, and slowly traces the letters with a finger. "This says 'M-O-M,' mom. My mom. She is my mom." The teacher emphasizes the initial sounds /m/ and /d/ for "mom" and "dad."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds up the word "DAD."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this 'mom' or 'dad'?" (Students point to the correct word or picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me 'sister'?" (Students point to the written word "SISTER" on the board or a flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large print picture book or a big poster with a short, simple story about "My Family." The text includes sentences like "This is my mom. She is kind. This is my dad. He is tall. This is my sister. She is young. This is my brother. He likes to play. I love my family." Each sentence is accompanied by a clear picture of the family member. The target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "She is," "He is," "my mom") are prominently featured.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Point to simple words in the text that identify objects or characters in a picture book; Recognize when written, without visual support, not only their own name but also the names of classmates or family members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, tracking the words with a pointer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Who is in this story?" (Students point to the pictures of family members as the teacher names them).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word Spotting:</b> "Point to 'mom' in the story." (The teacher guides students to touch the word "mom" on the poster/book). "Point to 'dad'." "Point to 'sister'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the written word "brother." "Find this word in the story!" Students come up and point to the word "brother" in the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading and recognizing family-related words (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather, pet) and short sentences ("She is my mom," "He is my dad," "My brother"). Practicing phonemic awareness for /b/ in "brother" and /s/ in "sister" by identifying words that start with these sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Read and Match" Worksheet.</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of family members on one side and the written words (e.g., "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister") on the other. Students draw lines to match each picture to its correct word. The teacher walks around, assisting and checking for accuracy. Teacher asks: "What letter does 'sister' start with?" (/s/). "What letter does 'brother' start with?" (/b/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Circle the Word."</b> The teacher displays flashcards or projects images of a family member. Below each image are two or three written word options (e.g., for a picture of a grandmother: "grandpa," "grandmother," "mom"). Students circle the correct word on their individual whiteboards or worksheets. Teacher guides: "This is a grandmother. Find 'grandmother' and circle it."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "My Name, Your Name."</b> Students work in pairs. Each student has a small card with their own name written on it and a card with a family word (e.g., "mom"). They show their "name" card to their partner and say, "This is [My Name]." Then they show the family word card and point to it, "This is 'mom'." The partner checks if they pointed to the correct word. They can swap cards or create more with "dad," "brother," etc.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Family Picture Book Reading."</b> Students work individually or in small groups (2-3 students). Each student receives a simple mini-picture book (either pre-made by the teacher or a blank booklet they can draw in). The book has 2-3 pages, each with a picture of a family member and a simple sentence below it (e.g., "This is my mom." "This is my brother." or "He is my dad."). Students first draw pictures of their own family members if using a blank booklet, then glue pre-printed word labels under each picture, or trace the words. Finally, they "read" their mini-book to a classmate by pointing to each word as they say it (or silently recognizing it), and identifying the family member in the picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> Students are actively reading and identifying written names of their own family members or common family roles, which directly relates to the "It's My Family!" theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in recognizing written family words, collaborative interaction (if reading to a partner), and communicative task completion (successfully "reading" their family book). They use "She is my mom," "He is my dad" phrases, and possessive "my."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to note student performance during the "My Family Picture Book Reading" task. The checklist includes student names and columns for criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "My Family Picture Book" to the teacher or a peer. They are asked to point to specific words in their book, such as "Point to 'mom'," "Point to 'brother'," or identify their own name in the text. The teacher observes their ability to correctly identify the words without visual cues beyond the context of the story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes own name when written.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies 3-4 target family words (e.g., mom, dad, brother, sister) in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matches words to correct pictures in their book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to pronounce the initial sounds of words like /b/ in "brother" and /s/ in "sister" when pointing.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a simple paper with two sentences and emojis:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read 'mom' and 'dad'." (Happy face / Neutral face / Sad face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find family words in a book." (Happy face / Neutral face / Sad face)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students circle the emoji that best describes their ability.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What family word did you read today that you like the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word was a little hard for you to read?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading activities worked best for identifying family words? Did all students successfully recognize their own names? How well did they connect the initial sounds (/b/, /s/) to the written words? I noticed that some students still relied heavily on pictures for word recognition. For next lesson, I need to ensure more varied activities that encourage students to produce these words orally while building on their reading recognition. I will prepare more visual aids for speaking prompts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on recognizing family words in written form. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively speak and interact using these recognized family words and structures (e.g., "She is my mom," "Who is he?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>